never persisted.  **Kanji first (ADR-040).** A new vocab card is never served before the new kanji cards of the kanji it requires. Those kanji cards come immediately before it, in the order of the word&#39;s kanji, as one **group**: the proportional mix never puts a review between them (only a learning card that falls due can come between; the group then continues right after it) and the whole group counts against the new-card goal. A group that does not fit today&#39;s remainder is not skipped and never overshoots the goal (ADR-043): as many of its kanji as fit are served today, and the word follows, behind its last kanji, on the first day the rest fits; nothing behind that word in its deck is introduced before it (a group may overshoot &#x60;limit&#x60;, never the goal). This holds for every word, whatever deck it comes from and for words in no deck; the deck setting &#x60;add_kanji&#x60; and the setting &#x60;scheduler.kanji_before_vocab&#x60; are no longer read. A kanji that already has a card in learning or review does not block and is not shown again; a suspended kanji card and a kanji note the user deleted are respected (the word comes without it); a kanji card the user **buried** holds its word back until the bury ends (ADR-043). A kanji card joins its word&#39;s group even when its own decks are all disabled. **Missing kanji notes are created here**, like deck entries (C-27): when the served build draws a word whose required kanji has no kanji note, the server creates the kanji note (&#x60;note.created&#x60; &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;auto_kanji&#x60;, built-in kanji type, and its default &#x60;card.created&#x60;s) in the same op transaction, before any vocab note of that fetch, and rebuilds; the word is withheld from &#x60;items&#x60; until its kanji exist. **Backfill:** the required kanji of words that are already being studied come first among the new cards, the oldest word&#39;s first: kanji cards that are still new, and (ADR-043) kanji that have no note yet, which are created in the same way. These kanji use at most half of the day&#39;s new goal (&#x60;floor(scheduler.new_per_day / 2)&#x60;, at least 1; ADR-044); the rest goes to new words with their own kanji, and when no new word can be drawn the kanji may use the remainder. Such a kanji card that is in no deck (every automatically created one) is drawn only through this share, never as an ordinary deckless new card; one that belongs to a deck is drawn through this share too (whatever the deck&#39;s priority or limit) and may also come up earlier as an ordinary card of its enabled deck. With &#x60;keep_going&#x3D;true&#x60; they are at most half of the new cards introduced in the study day. Example: goal 4, three such kanji and two new words → &#x60;日 本 うっかり Ｔシャツ&#x60; today, &#x60;語&#x60; on the next study day. Core &#x60;QueueBuilder&#x60; rule 4 \&quot;Kanji first\&quot; is normative. Example: new word 学校, no kanji notes, 3 reviews ready, 3 new left today → &#x60;R1 学 校 学校 R2 R3&#x60;.  **Which kanji a word requires (ADR-042, ADR-045, ADR-046).** Decided by the core predicate &#x60;KanjiRequirement&#x60; from the setting &#x60;cards.kanji_exemptions&#x60; (bool, default &#x60;true&#x60;; SCHEMA.md 6.5). While it is on: a word requires its **learnable** kanji only (jōyō, i.e. KANJIDIC grade 1-6 or 8, or KANJIDIC frequency rank 1-2500); a word whose displayed form is word-level tagged (JMdict &#x60;uk&#x60; on the sense used, or the kanji form tagged &#x60;ateji&#x60;, &#x60;rK&#x60;, &#x60;oK&#x60;, &#x60;iK&#x60; or &#x60;sK&#x60;) requires its **jōyō** kanji only (ADR-046). A word written with kanji shows its reading as furigana over the whole word on the front (&#x60;QueueItem.front_furigana&#x60;) when its displayed form is word-level tagged or when it requires no kanji. With the setting &#x60;false&#x60; every kanji is required and no front has furigana. A **kana word** (every kanji form of its entry is &#x60;rK&#x60; / &#x60;sK&#x60;; its &#x60;headword&#x60; is the reading) has no kanji either way. Examples: 寿司 (ateji) → 寿, 司 first, furigana front; 金糸雀 (ateji; 雀 is not jōyō) → 金, 糸 first, furigana front; 昨日 → 昨, 日 first, no furigana; 鱈 → no kanji card, furigana; シンガポール (forms 新嘉坡 … all &#x60;rK&#x60;) → front &#x60;シンガポール&#x60;, no kanji card.  **Old clients.** A client written before ADR-037 keeps working: &#x60;counts&#x60;, &#x60;more_available&#x60; and &#x60;next_learning_due_at&#x60; are still sent with their old meaning (see the schema). What it sees differently: the order (the proportional mix instead of the old interleave) and no per-session \&quot;done for now\&quot; (&#x60;scheduler.new_per_session&#x60; is ignored; &#x60;counts.new&#x60; is the day&#39;s remainder, never a session&#39;s).  **Deck entries become cards here (C-27, ADR-032).** Deck items &#x60;e:&lt;seq&gt;&#x60; with no note are new cards too (core &#x60;PendingEntry&#x60;; which entries qualify: contracts/core README \&quot;C-27\&quot;). The server passes them to the core builder, and when its build draws some (&#x60;toMaterialize&#x60; of the build that is **served** - &#x60;LoopResult.queue&#x60;; never the other build&#39;s - bounded by the same new-card budget as the cards: the day&#39;s remaining goal and the batch &#x60;limit&#x60;, or with keep going the batch alone; each entry counted as the whole kanji group its card will be, so an entry that would not fit is not drawn) and the caller holds &#x60;review&#x60; or &#x60;write&#x60;, the server creates them before answering, in one op transaction as the **server device**, exactly as contracts/core README \&quot;C-27\&quot; defines: first the kanji notes of the build&#39;s &#x60;kanjiToMaterialize&#x60; (ADR-040: the missing required kanji of every drawn word, whatever the deck&#39;s &#x60;add_kanji&#x60;; &#x60;note.created&#x60; &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;auto_kanji&#x60; + their &#x60;card.created&#x60;s; never a kanji the user deleted), then per drawn entry, in the builder&#39;s order, &#x60;note.created&#x60; {built-in vocab type, &#x60;entry_seq&#x60;, &#x60;fields&#x60; &#x60;{}&#x60;, &#x60;origin&#x60; &#x60;deck&#x60;} and one &#x60;card.created&#x60; per kind of the drawing deck&#39;s &#x60;card_kinds&#x60; (the vocab type&#39;s default kinds when that is absent, empty or invalid), minus kinds whose front would render empty. It then rebuilds and returns that queue; it materializes again (at most 3 rounds) only while the previous round&#39;s notes appear in &#x60;items&#x60;. This is the only place an API client&#39;s deck entries become notes: clients never mint these ops or call anything else for it; a device builds offline from the same core output and mints the same ops itself. - **Idempotent.** The transaction holds the sync lock and skips an entry a live note   already holds, so repeated or concurrent fetches never create a second note; a   fetch that draws nothing emits nothing (and takes no lock). - **Devices.** A device that created a note for the same entry offline converges by   SYNC.md 8.1 (the note that held the entry first survives; the other&#39;s cards and   reviews are merged into it). - **Read-only callers** (&#x60;read&#x60; only) get the queue without materializing: drawn   entries are counted in &#x60;to_materialize&#x60; (and the deprecated &#x60;counts.new&#x60;), not in   &#x60;items&#x60;; &#x60;loop.phase&#x60; is &#x60;reviewing&#x60; (rule C5). - &#x60;Kotobaya-Seq&#x60; is set when ops were emitted.  Each item carries everything needed to render and grade the card: the card with its note summary, the dictionary entry (when the note references one that exists), the example sentence chosen for rotating-example cards, the &#x60;review_type&#x60; an answer will be logged with, and a preview of the next due time per rating. Grammar cards also carry &#x60;grammar_sentence&#x60; (C-15): the rotated example sentence and span, chosen by the core picker with the rules on &#x60;GrammarCardSentence&#x60; (drill sessions use the same rules). 
         * @param limit Maximum number of cards to return. (optional, default to 20)
         * @param deckId Restrict new cards to this deck and reviews to cards whose note or entry is an item of it. Must be a live deck (&#x60;404&#x60; otherwise). (optional)
         * @param kinds Which queue sections to include, comma-separated. Default all three. (optional, default to setOf("new","learning","review"))
         * @param keepGoing C-26 (additive). &#x60;true&#x60; &#x3D; keep going past today&#39;s limits (see the description; core &#x60;QueueBuilder&#x60; rule 6, with &#x60;keepGoingNewBatch &#x3D; limit&#x60;). Default &#x60;false&#x60; &#x3D; the normal queue. Scope (ADR-039, rule C2 in the description): one review visit of one client, held in memory; every new visit, home screen and status fetch sends &#x60;false&#x60;. Per client, never synced. Only the literal values &#x60;true&#x60; and &#x60;false&#x60; are accepted; anything else is &#x60;422 validation_failed&#x60; with &#x60;errors[0].pointer &#x3D; /keep_going&#x60; and &#x60;code &#x3D; invalid_value&#x60;. Echoed as &#x60;loop.keep_going&#x60;. Older servers ignore the parameter (they return the normal queue and no &#x60;more_available&#x60;).  (optional, default to false)
         * @return ReviewQueue
        */
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
        open suspend fun getReviewQueue(limit: kotlin.Int? = 20, deckId: kotlin.String? = null, kinds: kotlin.collections.Set<kotlin.String>? = setOf("new","learning","review"), keepGoing: kotlin.Boolean? = false): HttpResponse<ReviewQueue> {

            val localVariableAuthNames = listOf<String>("sessionCookie", "bearerAuth")

            val localVariableBody = 
                    io.ktor.client.utils.EmptyContent

            val localVariableQuery = mutableMapOf<String, List<String>>()
            limit?.apply { localVariableQuery["limit"] = listOf("$limit") }
            deckId?.apply { localVariableQuery["deck_id"] = listOf("$deckId") }
            kinds?.apply { localVariableQuery["kinds"] = toMultiValue(this, "csv") }
            keepGoing?.apply { localVariableQuery["keep_going"] = listOf("$keepGoing") }

            val localVariableHeaders = mutableMapOf<String, String>()

            val localVariableConfig = RequestConfig<kotlin.Any?>(
            RequestMethod.GET,
            "/review/queue",
            query = localVariableQuery,
            headers = localVariableHeaders,
            requiresAuthentication = true,
            )

            return request(
            localVariableConfig,
            localVariableBody,
            localVariableAuthNames
            ).wrap()
            }

        /**
        * POST /review/undo
        * Undo a review.
        * Server emits &#x60;review.undone&#x60; for the review named by &#x60;review_id&#x60; and recomputes the card.  - &#x60;review_id&#x60; is **required** (any review, whichever device logged it, &#x60;manual&#x60; rows   included). There is no implicit \&quot;undo my last review\&quot;: that would not be idempotent   (a retry after a lost response would undo the review before it). The web app keeps the   &#x60;id&#x60; it sent to &#x60;POST /review/answer&#x60; and passes it here. - Target already undone: &#x60;200&#x60; with the current state, no op (an undo cannot be undone).   Hence retries are safe. - Unknown &#x60;review_id&#x60;: &#x60;404 not_found&#x60;. 
         * @param reviewUndoRequest  
         * @return ReviewResult
        */
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
        open suspend fun undoReview(reviewUndoRequest: ReviewUndoRequest): HttpResponse<ReviewResult> {

            val localVariableAuthNames = listOf<String>("sessionCookie", "bearerAuth")

            val localVariableBody = reviewUndoRequest

            val localVariableQuery = mutableMapOf<String, List<String>>()

            val localVariableHeaders = mutableMapOf<String, String>()

            val localVariableConfig = RequestConfig<kotlin.Any?>(
            RequestMethod.POST,
            "/review/undo",
            query = localVariableQuery,
            headers = localVariableHeaders,
            requiresAuthentication = true,
            )

            return jsonRequest(
            localVariableConfig,
            localVariableBody,
            localVariableAuthNames
            ).wrap()
            }

        }
